WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.667 --> 00:00:03.903
In this first lesson,
you'll look at the prompt format of Llama 3

2
00:00:03.903 --> 00:00:07.240
as well as some built
in capabilities of the model.

3
00:00:07.774 --> 00:00:10.076
I should also mention
that the set of notebooks

4
00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:14.314
you are going to implement in this course
is part of Meta's Llama recipes.

5
00:00:15.148 --> 00:00:17.684
This lesson
is also an overview of the steps

6
00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:20.553
you'll take to get your application ready
for production.

7
00:00:20.553 --> 00:00:22.822
Let's dive right in.

8
00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:25.992
So in lifting accuracy in LLMs,

9
00:00:25.992 --> 00:00:30.063
we're going to step back
and think about LLM apps, on their own.

10
00:00:30.063 --> 00:00:32.298
And they are really their own animal.

11
00:00:32.298 --> 00:00:37.070
ideally Llamas, these cute Llamas
because of LLMs, but they're their own

12
00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:37.504
animal,

13
00:00:37.504 --> 00:00:40.807
their own beast,
because they are highly probabilistic.

14
00:00:40.807 --> 00:00:41.241
Right?

15
00:00:41.241 --> 00:00:43.977
They're sampling
from a probability distribution.

16
00:00:43.977 --> 00:00:47.447
And that's why it can be a little bit
unpredictable what they're producing

17
00:00:47.447 --> 00:00:51.184
versus a deterministic system
where you're guaranteed a fixed output.

18
00:00:51.718 --> 00:00:56.189
And as a result, it's important to be
very iterative, in improving these models.

19
00:00:56.289 --> 00:00:58.258
You're not going to get it right
the first time with

20
00:00:58.258 --> 00:01:02.462
putting all the best practices together
like you do for a deterministic system.

21
00:01:02.796 --> 00:01:07.367
So deeply understanding and internalizing
even this iterative process

22
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.403
is extremely important
if you want to be able to lift accuracy

23
00:01:10.637 --> 00:01:13.773
to something like 95%,
and you'll actually get to do that

24
00:01:14.007 --> 00:01:18.178
in this course, which is really exciting,
on your own, for a specific application.

25
00:01:18.178 --> 00:01:22.715
Text to SQL, but also, one
that generalizes to your other LLM apps.

26
00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:26.052
And now I'm going to hand it over
to Amit,

27
00:01:26.052 --> 00:01:29.055
who will give you an overview of Llama 3.

28
00:01:29.422 --> 00:01:33.093
Llama models
built by Meta's research teams are large

29
00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:36.096
language models
based on transformer architecture.

30
00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:39.432
There are currently two Llama 3
models.

31
00:01:39.699 --> 00:01:42.502
There's a small 8 billion parameter model

32
00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:45.271
and a large 70 billion parameter model.

33
00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:47.740
In general, the larger the model is,

34
00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:51.044
the more capacity
it has to learn from its training data.

35
00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:54.314
However, larger models are also more

36
00:01:54.314 --> 00:01:57.884
computationally expensive to train
and deploy than the smaller models.

37
00:01:58.518 --> 00:02:01.588
Each of these models can be used
for different application scenarios

38
00:02:01.588 --> 00:02:03.022
and purposes.

39
00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:06.626
The instruction tune models are created
by taking the base models

40
00:02:06.793 --> 00:02:10.029
and running them through additional
training called instruction tuning.

41
00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.833
This enables instruction
tuned models to better follow

42
00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:18.833
human language instructions
such as summarize this or tell me a joke.

43
00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:23.076
These instruction tune models are called
Llama instruct models.

44
00:02:23.543 --> 00:02:25.178
Depending on your use case,

45
00:02:25.178 --> 00:02:27.080
you can take any of these models

46
00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:30.083
and further fine
tune them for your application needs.

47
00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:31.551
In this course,

48
00:02:31.551 --> 00:02:35.488
you will be using the Llama 3
8 billion parameter instruct model.

49
00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:37.457
The Llama 3

50
00:02:37.457 --> 00:02:40.460
family of models have industry
leading performance.

51
00:02:40.527 --> 00:02:43.163
Importantly, the family is open source.

52
00:02:43.163 --> 00:02:46.166
So you can modify these models
for your own applications.

53
00:02:46.666 --> 00:02:50.503
The Llama 3 8 billion parameter
model is used in many applications.

54
00:02:51.104 --> 00:02:54.107
It's small enough
that it can run on your laptop.

55
00:02:54.507 --> 00:02:57.844
It can have low latency,
making it useful in many tasks.

56
00:02:58.478 --> 00:03:01.214
In this course,
you will fine-tune this model to improve

57
00:03:01.214 --> 00:03:04.217
its performance
on generating database queries.

58
00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:05.218
All right.

59
00:03:05.218 --> 00:03:07.453
Let's start by importing our packages.

60
00:03:07.453 --> 00:03:10.456
We'll import the Lamini package.

61
00:03:10.623 --> 00:03:13.626
Then let's instantiate an LLM.

62
00:03:15.295 --> 00:03:18.264
We use the Llama 3,

63
00:03:18.264 --> 00:03:21.267
the 8 million parameter instruct model.

64
00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:24.537
Now let's create a prompt.

65
00:03:24.938 --> 00:03:28.541
Here's the beginning of text tag
and then the system header.

66
00:03:29.442 --> 00:03:31.744
And then the system message.

67
00:03:31.744 --> 00:03:34.414
You are a helpful assistant. Followed

68
00:03:34.414 --> 00:03:39.118
by the end of turn tag
and then the beginning of new header.

69
00:03:39.118 --> 00:03:39.919
The user header.

70
00:03:41.054 --> 00:03:41.988
Please write a birthday

71
00:03:41.988 --> 00:03:44.958
card for my good friend Andrew.

72
00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:47.260
End of turn.

73
00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:52.260
And a header for the assistant
which lets the LLM know its time

74
00:03:52.899 --> 00:03:54.067
to reply.

75
00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:57.070
Now let's call the LLM with that prompt.

76
00:03:59.973 --> 00:04:00.306
Here's

77
00:04:00.306 --> 00:04:03.343
a very nice birthday card
message for my friend Andrew.

78
00:04:04.477 --> 00:04:07.113
Happy birthday to an amazing friend
like you, Andrew.

79
00:04:07.113 --> 00:04:09.182
And so on.

80
00:04:09.182 --> 00:04:12.185
We have all been writing
long prompts for LLMs lately,

81
00:04:12.552 --> 00:04:15.555
and those are long strings in Python.

82
00:04:15.755 --> 00:04:19.926
Python's pep or Python enhancement
proposals

83
00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.563
PEP 8, gives you a style guide for Python,

84
00:04:23.563 --> 00:04:26.733
and it suggests that for long lines,

85
00:04:27.233 --> 00:04:32.233
they can be broken over multiple lines
by wrapping expressions in parentheses.

86
00:04:32.939 --> 00:04:35.875
Let's see how that's done.

87
00:04:35.875 --> 00:04:38.411
So here,
these strings are wrapped in parentheses

88
00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:42.815
and all of the individual
strings are concatenated together.

89
00:04:43.149 --> 00:04:45.285
And this makes a little easier to read.

90
00:04:45.285 --> 00:04:50.285
But a big advantage of this is the fact
that you can add comments to your strings.

91
00:04:50.723 --> 00:04:51.791
Let's try this out.

92
00:04:53.926 --> 00:04:56.129
You can see this looks similar
to our last one.

93
00:04:56.129 --> 00:04:59.065
Let's check to make sure that's true.

94
00:04:59.065 --> 00:05:02.001
Yeah, even though that looks nicer,

95
00:05:02.001 --> 00:05:05.004
we don't want to be writing those prompts
throughout the course.

96
00:05:05.405 --> 00:05:08.541
So let's create
a subroutine to do this for us.

97
00:05:09.008 --> 00:05:12.011
And we'll use this through
the rest of the course.

98
00:05:12.011 --> 00:05:16.449
This will be make Llama 3 prompt
and it will take in a user prompt

99
00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:21.449
and if you would like, a system prompt.
Let's build the system prompt. First,

100
00:05:22.455 --> 00:05:24.991
set it to an empty string.

101
00:05:24.991 --> 00:05:28.828
If the input which is a system
input, is not blank,

102
00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:32.632
then we will add this header.

103
00:05:32.965 --> 00:05:36.836
The start header system,
and then the system message

104
00:05:36.836 --> 00:05:40.073
you're passing
in, followed by the end of turn tag.

105
00:05:42.075 --> 00:05:44.243
Then we can write the rest of the prompt.

106
00:05:44.243 --> 00:05:47.980
So we have the beginning of the text
tag followed by the system prompt

107
00:05:48.448 --> 00:05:49.549
that we just created.

108
00:05:50.683 --> 00:05:53.686
The user
header, followed by the user message.

109
00:05:53.953 --> 00:05:56.989
And I've done tag
and then the assistant tag.

110
00:05:58.558 --> 00:06:00.326
And then we return the prompt.

111
00:06:00.326 --> 00:06:02.161
Let's try this out.

112
00:06:02.161 --> 00:06:05.031
That looks like the same prompt as before.

113
00:06:05.031 --> 00:06:08.034
But let's check it.

114
00:06:08.801 --> 00:06:09.302
Sure.

115
00:06:09.302 --> 00:06:10.837
Okay.

116
00:06:10.837 --> 00:06:13.639
Let's try this on a new example.

117
00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:16.476
Tell me a joke about birthday cakes.

118
00:06:16.476 --> 00:06:18.778
Notice that this one does
not have a system prompt,

119
00:06:18.778 --> 00:06:21.781
just a user prompt.

120
00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:24.317
Why was the birthday cake in a bad mood?

121
00:06:24.317 --> 00:06:26.352
Because it was feeling crumby.

122
00:06:26.352 --> 00:06:29.856
Now's a good time to pause
and try a few prompts on your own.

123
00:06:31.491 --> 00:06:34.494
The Llama family was trained
on a huge amount of data.

124
00:06:34.861 --> 00:06:38.197
As a result,
the model is able to generate SQL.

125
00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:43.564
We can use this capability to ask the
model questions about how to generate SQL.

126
00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:45.505
Let's try it out.

127
00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:48.241
We can ask the

128
00:06:48.241 --> 00:06:51.677
question given an arbitrary table
named SQL table

129
00:06:52.211 --> 00:06:55.581
right a query, to return
how many rows are in the table.

130
00:06:55.915 --> 00:06:59.218
Let's print out the result
of calling the LLM with that prompt.

131
00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:04.857
So, the model returns a description
of what it's returning,

132
00:07:04.924 --> 00:07:08.027
returns
the prompt and a little explanation

133
00:07:08.027 --> 00:07:11.030
of why it chose that response.

134
00:07:11.431 --> 00:07:13.666
We can ask more complicated questions.

135
00:07:13.666 --> 00:07:16.636
Let's try a few of these.

136
00:07:16.636 --> 00:07:19.605
Given an arbitrary table named SQL table,

137
00:07:19.605 --> 00:07:23.209
help me calculate the average height
where age is above 20.

138
00:07:26.245 --> 00:07:28.181
Great.

139
00:07:28.181 --> 00:07:32.418
Here we can see it's now selecting entries
where the age is greater than 20.

140
00:07:33.219 --> 00:07:36.222
We are not going to be executing these
in this lab.

141
00:07:36.289 --> 00:07:40.827
We are just exploring what the model is
able to generate. In the following labs,

142
00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:44.564
you will be generating these commands
and then executing them on a database.

143
00:07:46.766 --> 00:07:47.366
Let's try an

144
00:07:47.366 --> 00:07:50.369
even more complicated
example. Given that table,

145
00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:54.807
can you calculate the 95th percentile
height

146
00:07:55.208 --> 00:07:58.211
where the age is above 20?

147
00:07:58.978 --> 00:07:59.846
Perfect.

148
00:07:59.846 --> 00:08:03.115
So here's the SQL command to generate that.

149
00:08:05.117 --> 00:08:08.054
What if you are using SQLite.

150
00:08:08.054 --> 00:08:10.523
Let's try that same query.

151
00:08:10.523 --> 00:08:14.026
But this time we'll add use "SQLite".

152
00:08:16.829 --> 00:08:18.097
It returns the queries

153
00:08:18.097 --> 00:08:21.100
along with some hints
about how to use these.

154
00:08:21.367 --> 00:08:25.104
This is a good time to pause the video
and try a few queries of your own.

155
00:08:26.939 --> 00:08:28.341
Thank you. Amit.

156
00:08:28.341 --> 00:08:32.311
And next, we're going to go over, errors
we might see in the model.

157
00:08:32.311 --> 00:08:35.448
For example,
one common one is hallucinations.

158
00:08:35.848 --> 00:08:40.848
And, this is incorrect or made up
information that the model has generated.

159
00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:44.857
And it arises from the LLM thinking,
you know, something that's

160
00:08:44.857 --> 00:08:47.927
slightly right
is the same as it being right.

161
00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:49.896
Which in some cases is totally okay.

162
00:08:49.896 --> 00:08:53.299
So you might say hi or hello
and then that's totally

163
00:08:53.299 --> 00:08:56.636
okay if it interpreted those
or said one or the other to you,

164
00:08:56.769 --> 00:09:01.374
when you said hi back.
But for, let's say, your birthday,

165
00:09:01.407 --> 00:09:05.444
that is not okay to get wrong
or, specific fact, a revenue

166
00:09:05.444 --> 00:09:08.814
number, for example, in an earnings
report, these are not things

167
00:09:08.814 --> 00:09:13.586
where the slightly right answer
is the same as right for these facts.

168
00:09:13.819 --> 00:09:17.990
And this can be especially detrimental
for not just critical facts,

169
00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:19.692
but also when you just need precision.

170
00:09:19.692 --> 00:09:23.329
So when you're connecting to
a downstream system, like hitting an API,

171
00:09:23.462 --> 00:09:27.733
or you're connecting to certain IDs,
this is, this is especially important.

172
00:09:27.733 --> 00:09:30.503
And so as an example, Llama 3
might produce

173
00:09:30.503 --> 00:09:34.840
this beautiful SQL statement
and you're hitting a SQLite database.

174
00:09:35.107 --> 00:09:38.277
But the hallucination here
is that while this might run,

175
00:09:38.277 --> 00:09:41.414
this might execute in an MySQL, database.

176
00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:45.184
It won't actually run in SQL
lite because percentile_cont

177
00:09:45.184 --> 00:09:47.720
is actually not available in SQLite.

178
00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:49.689
So this will fail.
This is an invalid query.

179
00:09:50.823 --> 00:09:51.090
Okay.

180
00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:52.425
So how would you tackle this?

181
00:09:52.425 --> 00:09:55.761
Well, the most common approach
is obviously just prompt engineering.

182
00:09:55.761 --> 00:09:58.164
It's the easiest, lightest lift
that you can do.

183
00:09:58.164 --> 00:10:01.000
For a case like this though,
prompt engineering

184
00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:04.837
we found, often gets to something
like 20 to 30%.

185
00:10:05.237 --> 00:10:09.275
Adding some self-reflection can sometimes
help the model, lift it up to maybe 40%.

186
00:10:09.609 --> 00:10:13.212
And then retrieval augmented generation
or RAG, get it up to 50%.

187
00:10:13.479 --> 00:10:15.815
and instruction
fine-tuning, you know, mixed results

188
00:10:15.815 --> 00:10:19.018
from, you know, something that's under RAG
or a little bit over RAG.

189
00:10:19.018 --> 00:10:20.586
It can be pretty difficult
to get the model

190
00:10:20.586 --> 00:10:23.923
to commit to sampling
or producing the right results.

191
00:10:24.423 --> 00:10:25.358
And why is that?

192
00:10:25.358 --> 00:10:28.594
So let's actually look into technically
what's going on in this model.

193
00:10:28.961 --> 00:10:30.096
So you might ask,

194
00:10:30.096 --> 00:10:33.599
you know, what year did Dave Aguilar climb
the Golden Gate Bridge.

195
00:10:33.866 --> 00:10:37.703
And you might RAG up a Wikipedia article
about the Golden Gate Bridge.

196
00:10:38.137 --> 00:10:40.940
And, the model is prepared
to answer the question.

197
00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:44.010
It says he climbed it in
and it's going to say a date.

198
00:10:44.176 --> 00:10:47.647
And, this often might work
because I might say 1981,

199
00:10:47.647 --> 00:10:50.650
which is the correct answer,
but it sometimes fails.

200
00:10:50.816 --> 00:10:52.018
And what's going on here?

201
00:10:52.018 --> 00:10:55.021
Well, let's take a look
at this probability distribution here.

202
00:10:55.021 --> 00:10:57.256
It's sampling in this probability
space.

203
00:10:57.256 --> 00:10:59.525
It's not going to sample
cat here on the edge. Right.

204
00:10:59.525 --> 00:11:00.626
Not very likely.

205
00:11:00.626 --> 00:11:02.094
But among the dates among things

206
00:11:02.094 --> 00:11:06.132
that are similar,
it is going to consider sampling there.

207
00:11:06.132 --> 00:11:07.299
And that's why it fails.

208
00:11:07.299 --> 00:11:09.769
It's because it's
considering, things that are close.

209
00:11:09.769 --> 00:11:12.772
and as a result, it's not going to say
he climbed it in cat.

210
00:11:13.139 --> 00:11:16.509
But it will say, you know,
he climbed it in 1970,

211
00:11:16.709 --> 00:11:19.979
which is, hallucination
and in some ways more detrimental

212
00:11:19.979 --> 00:11:23.916
than even saying cat because,
you know, it, it you don't know.

213
00:11:23.916 --> 00:11:25.418
It doesn't know.

214
00:11:25.418 --> 00:11:28.654
So the question is then, given
all those techniques that,

215
00:11:28.688 --> 00:11:32.291
that don't fully work
and reducing all these hallucinations,

216
00:11:32.591 --> 00:11:35.127
how can fine-tuning actually help?

217
00:11:35.127 --> 00:11:36.295
one way it can help

218
00:11:36.295 --> 00:11:40.199
is that there is a method of fine-tuning
where you can embed facts into the model.

219
00:11:40.199 --> 00:11:42.835
but instruction fine-tuning,
which is the most common form of fine

220
00:11:42.835 --> 00:11:46.305
tuning, isn't actually that tool
to remove those hallucinations.

221
00:11:46.305 --> 00:11:49.075
And it can be kind of costly as well.

222
00:11:49.075 --> 00:11:52.311
There's a technique called memory
tuning invented by Lamini,

223
00:11:52.445 --> 00:11:55.781
which lets the model
actually recall these facts precisely

224
00:11:55.781 --> 00:11:58.517
by embedding the facts
directly into the weights of the model,

225
00:11:58.517 --> 00:12:01.520
and actually inject
a little bit of determinism into this,

226
00:12:01.754 --> 00:12:03.456
very probabilistic process.

227
00:12:03.456 --> 00:12:07.193
And one important thing about this is,
you might ask,

228
00:12:07.193 --> 00:12:10.396
okay, well, are you just going to overfit
the model on everything?

229
00:12:10.396 --> 00:12:13.499
One important thing is we're not right,
because otherwise you lose

230
00:12:13.499 --> 00:12:16.669
the magic of
why we're using LLMs in the first place.

231
00:12:16.936 --> 00:12:20.973
So the goal is to be extremely precise
on those facts

232
00:12:20.973 --> 00:12:24.143
without compromising the generalization
and instruction

233
00:12:24.143 --> 00:12:26.345
following that, we absolutely love.

234
00:12:26.345 --> 00:12:29.982
And memory tuning
is able to lift us up to 95% accuracy.

235
00:12:30.149 --> 00:12:33.953
And the reason why is because
when we ask this question again,

236
00:12:33.953 --> 00:12:37.857
the model is, sampling from a distribution
that looks more like

237
00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:42.890
this, essentially sampling from only 1981
being the only possible response.

238
00:12:43.696 --> 00:12:46.532
And the way this happens,
which we'll go over in greater detail,

239
00:12:46.532 --> 00:12:50.269
is to bring the what we call loss
to zero on these facts.

240
00:12:50.669 --> 00:12:50.970
Okay.

241
